export default {"id":"bassstation-level-8","number":8,"title":"Mixer Section (6-into-1 Source Management)","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"- Balance the three dedicated primary tone generators (Oscillator 1, Oscillator 2, and Sub-Oscillator) into the analogue summing mixer. - Master the multiplexed operation of the shared Noise / Ring / Ext potentiometer and selector switch, v","modules":[{"id":"bassstation-m-8-1","code":"8","title":"Mixer Section (6-into-1 Source Management)","blurb":"- Balance the three dedicated primary tone generators (Oscillator 1, Oscillator 2, and Sub-Oscillator) into the analogue summing mixer. - Master the multiplexed operation of the shared Noise / Ring / Ext potentiometer and selector switch, v","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"bassstation-l-o-8","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 8.1 — Six Sources into One Analogue Summing Bus","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Section Learning Outcomes","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Balance the three dedicated primary tone generators (<strong>Oscillator 1</strong>, <strong>Oscillator 2</strong>, and <strong>Sub-Oscillator</strong>) into the analogue summing mixer.","Master the multiplexed operation of the shared <strong>Noise / Ring / Ext</strong> potentiometer and selector switch, verifying non-destructive parameter memory retention.","Utilize <strong>Ring Modulation</strong> as an independent mixer source for harmonic and clangorous sound design.","Route and calibrate external audio through the rear <code>EXT IN</code> socket, utilizing the digital <strong>Input Gain</strong> On-Key function (-10 dB to +60 dB)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-8-1-1","title":"8.1.1: Dedicated Primary Source Level Control","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Mixer section serves as the analog summing junction that feeds the discrete filter and overdrive circuitry. The upper row provides three dedicated, continuous rotary potentiometers:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      MIXER SUMMING JUNCTION TOPOLOGY\n   [Oscillator 1]     ──► [OSC 1 Level Pot (0–127)] ──┐\n   [Oscillator 2]     ──► [OSC 2 Level Pot (0–127)] ──┼──► [Analogue Summing Bus]\n   [Sub-Oscillator]   ──► [SUB Level Pot (0–127)]   ──┤              │\n   [Noise Generator]  ──┐                             │              ▼\n   [Ring Modulator]   ──┼─► [Shared Pot (0–127)]   ───┘     [Analogue Filter Input]\n   [External Input]   ──┘   (Noise/Ring/Ext Select)</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Gain Staging &amp; Headroom</strong>:","Each potentiometer scales the audio level from <code>0</code> (complete attenuation/mute) to <code>127</code> (maximum gain).","Setting all three primary oscillators to <code>127</code> drives the analog summing amp into soft saturation before entering the filter.","For cleaner, more delicate acoustic or jazz sounds, setting individual oscillator levels between <code>60</code> and <code>90</code> preserves headroom and minimizes inter-oscillator harmonic clipping."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load Init patch <code>64</code>. Set <strong>Osc 1</strong> to 100, <strong>Osc 2</strong> to 100 (detuned +8 cents), and <strong>Sub</strong> to 80.","Play a low baseline note; observe the rich, thick analog sum.","Lower Osc 1 and Osc 2 to 0, leaving only the Sub-Oscillator; observe that the Sub is completely isolated."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-8-1-2","title":"8.1.2: Shared Control Workflow & Independent Memory Retention","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The lower row of the Mixer features a single physical potentiometer alongside a three-position slide switch labelled <strong>Noise / Ring / Ext</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                 SHARED MIXER CONTROL MULTIPLEXING\n   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │ [Noise / Ring / Ext Switch]                                 │\n   │   ├── Position 1: NOISE (White Noise Generator)             │\n   │   ├── Position 2: RING (Osc 1 × Osc 2 Ring Modulator)       │\n   │   └── Position 3: EXT (Rear Panel ¼\" Audio Input)           │\n   │                                                             │\n   │ [Shared Potentiometer] ──► Updates the selected register    │\n   │ *Rule*: Unselected registers preserve their stored values.  │\n   └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Parameter Isolation Logic</strong>:","The microprocessor maintains three distinct digital volume registers in memory: <code>Level_Noise</code>, <code>Level_Ring</code>, and <code>Level_Ext</code>.","The physical potentiometer adjusts only the register currently selected by the slide switch.","<em>Example</em>: If you set the switch to <strong>Noise</strong> and turn the knob to <code>100</code>, then flip the switch to <strong>Ring</strong> and turn the knob to <code>0</code>, <strong>the Noise level remains at <code>100</code> in memory</strong>. Switching back to Noise will not zero out your noise volume.","<strong>Original Value Feedback</strong>:","When switching between Noise, Ring, and Ext, the Original Value LEDs (<code>◄</code> and <code>►</code>) indicate whether the physical knob position is above or below the stored value for that specific source."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Mute Osc 1, Osc 2, and Sub (set to 0).","Set the switch to <strong>Noise</strong> and set the knob to maximum (127). Confirm white noise is audible.","Flip the switch to <strong>Ring</strong> and set the knob to zero (0). Confirm silence.","Flip the switch to <strong>Ext</strong> and set the knob to zero (0). Confirm silence.","Flip the switch back to <strong>Noise</strong>. Strike a key; confirm that the noise generator is still fully sounding at maximum volume."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] If you adjust the shared knob while the switch is set to Ring, does it alter the volume of the Noise generator?","[ ] How does the synthesizer remember three different volume levels using a single physical knob?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-8-1-3","title":"8.1.3: Ring Modulation as an Independent Source","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Source Generation</strong>:","Ring modulation is produced by analog four-quadrant multiplication of the real-time waveforms of <strong>Oscillator 1</strong> and <strong>Oscillator 2</strong>.","Unlike digital synthesizers where ring mod is a master effect, the Bass Station II treats the ring modulator output as a completely independent audio channel in the mixer.","<strong>Layering Techniques</strong>:","You can blend clean saw/sine waveforms from Osc 1 and Osc 2 alongside the ring-modulated signal, allowing sound designers to anchor musical fundamental pitch while adding subtle metallic grit or aggressive industrial sidebands."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Osc 1 to <strong>8' Sawtooth</strong> (level 90 in mixer).","Set Osc 2 to <strong>16' Square</strong> (level 0 in mixer, but tuned to a Fifth with Coarse <code>+7</code>).","Set the shared mixer switch to <strong>Ring</strong> and bring the level up to <code>70</code>.","Play sustained notes; hear a harmonically grounded lead tone layered with complex metallic overtones."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"bassstation-l-8-1-4","title":"8.1.4: External Audio Input & Digital Input Gain Calibration","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Technical Exposition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The rear panel <code>EXT IN</code> socket allows external instruments to be injected into the Bass Station II's analog signal path."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">                      EXTERNAL AUDIO INJECTION PATH\n   [External Device] (e.g., Drum Machine / Guitar Preamp)\n          │\n          ▼\n   [Rear EXT IN Jack (¼\" TS)]\n          │\n          ▼ (Digital Input Gain Calibration: -10 dB to +60 dB via On-Key)\n   [Mixer: Ext Level Control] ──► [Classic/Acid Filter] ──► [Distortion] ──► [VCA]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Input Sensitivity &amp; Gain Range</strong>:","Different audio sources output vastly different signal voltages (e.g., consumer line level -10 dBV, professional studio line level +4 dBu, high-impedance instrument levels).","The Bass Station II features a dedicated digital pre-amplifier for the external input calibrated via the On-Key <strong>Input Gain</strong> function.","Range: <strong>−10 dB to +60 dB</strong> in 1 dB increments (Default = <code>0 dB</code>).","The configured Input Gain setting is <strong>saved globally across power cycles</strong>.","<strong>Triggering Requirements</strong>:","External audio entering <code>EXT IN</code> passes through the analog filter and VCA.","To hear external audio, the VCA must be opened. This can be accomplished by:","Holding down a key on the keybed.","Engaging the <strong>Latch</strong> button in the Arp section (Key Hold mode).","Sending continuous MIDI Note-On commands from an external sequencer."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Hardware Verification & Practical Exercise","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Connect an external audio source (e.g., drum loop from a phone or audio interface) to the rear <code>EXT IN</code> jack.","Press <strong>Function / Exit</strong>, then press the <strong>Input Gain</strong> key (the upper <code>C</code> key).","The display shows <code>0</code>. Use <strong>Patch/Value ▲ and ▼</strong> to adjust the digital preamp gain to match the incoming signal level without clipping. Exit Function mode.","Set the shared mixer switch to <strong>Ext</strong> and raise the level to 100.","Press the <strong>Latch</strong> button (Key Hold); sweep the <strong>Filter Frequency</strong> and <strong>Resonance</strong> dials to filter the external drum track through the analog circuitry."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"3. Self-Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["[ ] What is the adjustable gain range for the external input preamplifier?","[ ] Is the Input Gain setting saved in memory after power is turned off?","[ ] Why must a key be pressed or Latched to hear external audio fed into EXT IN?"],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
